          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>What are the best easy hikes near Philadelphia for beginners?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              The Piedmont hills just south of Wilmington — Ebright Azimuth, Horseshoe Hill and Drummond
              Hill — are the easiest and closest, all under 45 minutes from Philadelphia with modest
              elevation gain and clearly marked trails.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Do I need a parking pass for hiking trails near Philadelphia?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Most Delaware state park trailheads require a small day-use pass in season. New Jersey
              state forest and Worthington/Delaware Water Gap National Recreation Area trailheads are
              largely free, though some fill up early on fall weekends. Check each trail's parking
              information page for specifics.
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="group">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 bg-stone-50 rounded-xl hover:bg-stone-100 transition-colors font-bold text-stone-900 list-none">
              <span>Can I hike near Philadelphia in winter?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-600 transform transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="p-4 text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
              Yes — the Delaware Piedmont trails stay walkable through most of winter with basic traction.
              The Kittatinny Ridge and Mount Tammany can ice over on north-facing sections, so bring
              microspikes and check conditions before heading up in December through March.
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Hiking Tips for Philadelphia Area -->
      <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-50 to-blue-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-emerald-200">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-6">Essential Tips for Hiking Near Philadelphia</h2>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-2xl">🚗</span> Getting There
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-stone-700">
              <li>• Delaware's Piedmont hills are reachable via I-95 S in under an hour</li>
              <li>• For the Kittatinny Ridge and NJ Highlands, take I-78 E or Route 202 N</li>
              <li>• For Mount Tammany, take I-80 W to the Delaware Water Gap exit</li>
              <li>• Download offline maps in case of poor cell service on back roads</li>
            </ul>
